welcome to the Macmillan report I'm  Marilyn Wilkes your host and our guest  is Deborah Brautigan a leading expert on  China in Africa and the Bernard L  Schwartz professor of international  political economy at Johns Hopkins  University her teaching and research  focus on international development  strategies governance and foreign aid  and she regularly advises international  agencies and governments on China Africa  economic engagement professor Brautigan  is the author of the dragon's gift the  real story of China in Africa and  Chinese aid and African Development  exporting Green Revolution she is with  us today because she is giving the  annual coca-cola world fund at Yale  lecture at the Macmillan Center and we  are delighted to be able to talk with  her about her new book will Africa feed  China  welcome professor of rottingham thanks  Maryland it's great to be here so let's  begin with the book tell us about it  the book is called will Africa feed  China and it examines the conventional  wisdom on what we think that Chinese are  doing in Africa with regard to  agricultural engagement and I think that  conventional wisdom really has four  points people think that the Chinese  have acquired a large amount of land  they think that the Chinese government  is leading this effort they think that  the purpose is to grow food to send back  to China and they think that they're  sending a lot of Chinese farmers to do  that ok so what led you to write the  book  well my first book was actually about  Chinese agricultural engagement in  Africa that was one about Chinese aid  and African development so I wrote that  book in the 1990s and I've been  following what the Chinese are doing for  a long time and it seemed to me that we  were really getting a lot wrong on what  they were doing and really  misunderstanding it and I kept seeing  these headlines about what they were  doing and I kept saying to myself this  isn't accurate and so I decided to dig  into this more deeply I had looked  the issue of agriculture in the Dragons  gift I had a couple of chapters on that  but it was really it really needed a  whole book to itself okay so it was  fascinating to actually discover what  the Chinese we're doing in Africa and  how little resemblance that bore to what  we thought they were doing interesting  interesting  so the the Chinese have been in Africa  for four decades then at this point  mm-hmm okay so how did you do the  methodology what was the research that  you did to write the book well I really  had a head start because I first went to  Africa to look at what the Chinese were  doing in the rural areas in 1983 so I  already had a lot of experience and I  knew a lot of cases of their foreign aid  and I also knew that over the years many  of those foreign aid projects had turned  into investments because they were often  done as state farms for African  governments so for this book we I worked  with a team of people I worked with  students and colleagues who were also  doing field work and we basically did  three things first of all we did what I  call internet investigative sleuthy and  so this is a kind of forensic internet  investigation where I and my students  dig into cases online to try to find out  what we can about them and the second  was we did fieldwork so we visited as  many cases as we could of investments  that it actually happened or those that  had failed and then tried to find out  what had happened and third we looked  very carefully at the Chinese government  incentive structure what were the  policies how did they go about trying to  encourage investment and what could we  find out from that okay so what are some  of the myths about China's agricultural  interest in Africa what are some of the  big ones well let's take for example the  idea that the Chinese have acquired  large amounts of farmland in Africa if  the Chinese had actually acquired all of  the farmland that people believe they  have out of all of the media stories and  all of the examples that are out there  it would be about six million hectares  and this is huge it's about 1% of all of  the arable land in Africa so it's quite  quite a lot of land and we found by  looking into sixty cases which we had  collected for  all different sources and trying to find  out what actually happened in each of  those that the amount of land that's  been transferred to Chinese companies is  240 around 240 thousand hectares which  is about twice the size of New York City  so it's a lot less land than people  think right do you have any idea as to  why these myths come about this one in  particular I think the idea that Africa  might be feeding China this is not  something that people that really know  Africa probably believe because Africa  is a big food deficit region every year  for example they're importing about 10  million tons of rice so that's an  enormous amount five to six billion  dollars they by and large most countries  our food deficit and so the idea that  they would be feeding China when they  can't feed themselves is very  unrealistic however I do think people  that don't know that might look at the  amount of land that people have in China  very little the amount of people they  have there very many and then Africa  seems to be a vast area with a lot of  land and so it seems like a natural that  the Chinese would want to grow their  food there right so they're just  basically making up stories that are not  true well they're doing things like  they're looking at a Chinese company  comes and they inquire about getting  some land I'll give you one example in  Zambia there was a real project at least  they wanted it to be a project a Chinese  company came they asked about doing a  very large project that would have been  biofuels and then this was reported as  though the Chinese government had come  and had asked for land to grow food to  send back to China and the whole idea of  this project was going to be to grow  biofuels which could be turned into fuel  and that they would sell this to the  Zambians because the Zambia was putting  in a requirement that they have a  certain percentage of ethanol in their  fuel and so this company thought they  could supply that so it was a the  intention the company wanted and and the  way it was spun when it got into the  media were entirely different  interesting so tell us about some some  other myths  well another myth is a Africa is  actually exporting food to China already  we looked at the trade  in order to figure this one out and what  we found is that there are a lot of  agricultural exports coming from Africa  but by and large it's things like  tobacco it's cotton it's things that are  going into industrial production like  rubber  there is one food commodity that's in  the top five  and that's sesame seeds so I don't think  that one could say that Chinese have a  voracious appetite for african sesame  they're trying to lock that up so that  they can feed themselves with it but  sesame is a very competitive crop in  Africa and the Chinese have come there  to buy it so they're buying it from  African farmers and again you know I am  curious how these myths happen and it's  just basically reporting that's not  quite accurate yes is that what it  amounts to I mean I think we developed a  preconceived idea that this would be  what the Chinese would want to do even  though they hadn't said they would want  to do that they were not sending  companies to do it the main examples of  industrial interests that we found were  in things like rubber or oil palm  biofuels it wasn't in growing food and  it certainly wasn't in growing food to  send back to China so we really we were  a lot of the interest in what the  Chinese were doing  crystallized during the last food  security that when global prices were  rising very sharply around 2008 and so  because there was a lot of investor  interest in in acquiring land because it  looked as though it was becoming more  valuable and then people worried about  food security and they thought that the  Chinese were getting quite worried about  food security so these things came  together and they just interpreted  investor interest from China as being  about food security and it's China in  virtually every African country or just  specific ones if you're talking about  China as an investor or China as a aid  partner they're just about everywhere  as an investor as some as where their  companies are seeking business they're  really everywhere even in places like  Somalia if you're talking about  diplomatically there are three countries  that still recognize Taiwan so there's  no Chinese government presence there but  in terms of Chinese companies bidding on  infrastructure projects they're really  interested in getting that business all  over the continent and how is Africa  responding to China's presence there are  they liking it are there issues  maryland's hard to summarize 54  countries but we do have some public  opinion data of that and so there are a  number of groups including the BBC and  afro barometer and the pew pew  charitable trust as a survey on public  opinion and what we can see from that is  that the opinion about the Chinese is it  varies country by country in some  countries it's quite positive others  it's less so South Africa for example  it's very negative and then we compare  this with the United States and what's  interesting is that on average the  public opinion is very similar and we're  more popular the u.s. is more popular  than the Chinese but in general on  average across a continent both China  and the United States are regarded  favorably and so I think with China the  main concern tends to be not so much  about the Chinese sending farmers there  because they aren't actually doing that  but it's about labor relations and this  is the thing I hear most whether it's  agriculture or mining or manufacturing  Africans are concerned about the  treatment that they get from Chinese  investors who have very different  cultural expectations so that's a real  blot on the Chinese in Africa it's a  problem it's a challenge and there are  others as well the environment it's  another issue I hear about but on the  other hand people are really positive  about the infrastructure that's being  built by Chinese companies so there are  good things and there are challenges  I'd say in this relationship and it's  growing very very rapidly right ok any  other message you'd like to tell us  about I think the last one that is  really interesting is this idea that  China is sending  a million farmers girl food in Africa  and I've seen this in several different  ways I've even seen it said to be a  hundred million that they want to  sandwich to Africa and so again there's  this idea of so many people and so much  land in Africa that's available and what  we found which is interesting is that  the small-scale farmers and there are  small-scale farmers from China growing  food and other crops in Africa almost  totally for local markets they're  supplying Chinese restaurants they're  supplying some African markets you can  now buy Chinese vegetables in in many  African markets things that you couldn't  buy before but these are not Chinese  farmers actually they are Chinese  entrepreneurs that have looked at  farming as a profitable niche investment  that they could make and so very few if  any of them actually come from a farming  background in China instead they're  businessman interesting all right let's  talk realities now Ryota are some of the  realities of China being in Africa other  than the ones that you've already  pointed out mmm well there are many  realities  I think trade is part of the reality and  this is something that in the u.s. we  look at with with some concern because  our export market in Africa's it's  complementary right now with China's  export market but it's it's relatively  small for example I think we're  exporting about 6070 billion or so maybe  even less so our total trade is probably  under a hundred million and so exports  would probably be less but Chinese  exports and Chinese trade with Africa's  right now about 220 billion dollars  annually so they're by far the largest  bilateral and bilateral trading partner  for the continent and they're exporting  a lot and one of the things they're  exporting is manufactured goods so in  many African countries another area of  tension is that Chinese goods coming in  and compete with local manufactured  goods so in South Africa that's very  much the case we also see this in Egypt  and in Nigeria in particular so people  that work in factories in those  countries are concerned  about having to compete with the Chinese  and this is going to be even tougher  because recently the Chinese devalued  their currency and so that makes their  experts even more competitive and harder  to compete with do you think the African  government will step in and shank  sanction some of them well this is  interesting Maryland because during the  1980s and 1990s African countries were  asked by the World Bank and the IMF to  liberalize their trade markets and so  they have they've opened their borders  to imports from outside and now they're  finding that it's the Chinese that are  supplying those imports that wasn't  quite what the intention was perhaps but  we have this idea that free trade is  always best at least that's the  Washington Consensus where where I come  from in Washington and that it becomes  very difficult to industrialize if you  have to open your borders to imports so  how do you actually move up the value  chain and get your factories to be able  to produce if they have no protection or  very little protection so I think what's  happening somewhat in Africa is that  they're starting to rethink this whole  idea of free trade and wonder how can  they protect their industries from China  and then try to move up the value chain  that way and I think that's a very  interesting conversation that's ongoing  ok and what do you think the trend will  be for China and Africa moving forward  any predictions I do have some  predictions I do think it's going to be  leveling off the intense interest that  we saw particularly from about 2004 to  about 2009 I looked at the trade figures  recently and trade was growing at over  40% annually during that period this is  a huge increase and then from 2009 to  2014 trade grew at under 15% per year so  that's a big settling down and so I  think that kind of suddenly down will be  the trend in the future we're not going  to see those huge increases that we saw  about 10 years ago and why do you think  it's settling down just because there's  more goods in the country at this point  people aren't buying them what do you  what do you think is the rationale for  that it will settle down for two reasons  one is that African economies  also growing more slowly and so that  means that they have less ability to  import things from China so that  consumption side will slow down and on  the side of the Chinese they their  economy is also slowing and so they have  less need for the commodities that  they've been importing from Africa so  that trade will slow down as well and  then when you have less need that's less  price pressure and so the prices of all  those commodities will slow which means  the total value of the trade will slow  and we already see this happening okay  and in what you conclude in your book I  conclude may be surprisingly given  everything I've said today but I do  think that Africa should feed China it's  not at present I don't see it happening  in the foreseeable future but the fact  that they have so much land that is not  being productively used the fact that so  many parts of the continent have not yet  really adopted commercial agriculture I  do think there are things they could  learn from the way the Chinese invited  in for an investment into their own  agricultural sector and there are a lot  of examples of that in my book but I  also saw in my research that in the past  some parts of Africa had exported quite  a bit for example Zimbabwe before they  had this disastrous land reform they  used to export to Asia to Indonesia  Malaysia Japan and even to China so they  were the breadbasket of southern Africa  and I think that could happen again  possibly it's it's a challenge to get  agricultural development going and to  commercialize it is a challenge because  right now in many parts of the continent  you have subsistence agriculture you  have basically regions where capitalism  has hardly entered and so people are  subs are producing for their own  families and maybe a little bit extra  for the market and the land is not being  used efficiently and yet these are  people with with very real needs very  poor people and taking their land away  which is what's happened everywhere else  in the world that this kind of  subsistence farming this is doesn't  exist in very many places anymore this  is a very painful transition so everyone  wants at least well-meaning people want  to make this to be less painful and  want to help these small farmers to move  up the value chain my own view on that  is that some areas this might work but  in a lot of parts of the continent it's  unrealistic the small farmers are not  going to be the future in many parts of  Africa they're going to move to the  cities they're going to look for jobs  there and they will have to  commercialize on a larger scale but this  is still very controversial and I don't  think it's been accepted by a lot of  Africa's partners as well as by a lot of  Africans themselves ok very interesting  thank you so much for being here with us  today and sharing some of your work it's  been a pleasure thank you for having me  for more information about professor  Brautigan and her research please visit  our website at macmillan report yale.edu  be sure to join us again for another  episode of the Macmillan report made  possible through funding from the  Whitney and Betty Macmillan Centre for  International and area studies at Yale

